/*! \page running_example_docker_exp Launching Engines in Docker Containers Examples

The folder `"examples"` contains four examples (listed in the table below) of how to use the \ref docker_launcher "DockerLauncher launch command" to launch Engine processes in docker containers.
In each of these experiments there is a `simulation_config_docker.json` configuration file which uses DockerLauncher to launch and run one of the engines in the experiment in a docker container.

<table>
<tr><th>Example folder<th>Description<th>ImageName<th>Local build command
<tr><td>husky_braitenberg<td>Running a gazebo_grpc engine with a husky model inside a docker container<td>nrp-local/nrp-gazebo:local<td>./build_nrp_core_image.sh nrp-gazebo
<tr><td>nest_simple<td>Running a nest_json engine with a simple nest model inside a docker container<td>nrp-local/nrp-nest-gazebo:local<td>./build_nrp_core_image.sh nrp-nest-gazebo
<tr><td>pysim_examples/opensim_control<td>Running a pysim engine with a arm26 model of OpenSim inside a docker container<td>nrp-local/nrp-opensim:local<td>./build_nrp_core_image.sh nrp-opensim
<tr><td>tf_exchange<td>Running a python_json engine inside a docker container<td>nrp-local/nrp-vanilla:local<td>./build_nrp_core_image.sh nrp-vanilla
</table>

The `ImageName` values are the tags produced by the local image build, i.e. `build_nrp_core_image.sh <service>` run with the `.env.template` defaults `NRP_DOCKER_REGISTRY=nrp-local` and `NRP_CORE_TAG=local`.
The service names and the layered build are described in \ref docker_compose "Building NRPCore Docker images". The image set targets Ubuntu 22.04 (jammy) with ROS 2 Humble and Python 3.10 only.

Unlike the docker-compose example files, the DockerLauncher configuration does not substitute `${NRP_DOCKER_REGISTRY}` and `${NRP_CORE_TAG}`: `ImageName` is passed literally to the docker daemon.
DockerLauncher never builds an image. If the image is not present on the daemon's host, the docker SDK tries to pull it from the registry implied by the name; for the `nrp-local` namespace that pull fails (it is not a registry) and the launch aborts with "The specified docker image ... could not be found".
So either run the build command from the table first (the docker-compose runner `examples/run_docker-compose_example.sh` builds missing images itself, DockerLauncher does not), or edit `ImageName` in `simulation_config_docker.json` to name a published image, which is then pulled automatically.
Of the runtime variants only `hbpneurorobotics/nrp-vanilla` and `hbpneurorobotics/nrp-nest-gazebo` are published on Docker Hub (the `latest` and `development` tags follow the `development` branch); `nrp-gazebo` and `nrp-opensim` have to be built locally.

To run the experiments, users should first, on the host where Engines are to be launched in docker containers, setup docker daemon properly.
See this \ref configuring_docker_daemon "guide" for more information on how to do this.

Once this is done, on the host where the experiment is launched, one should run the experiment as usual. For example:

\code{.sh}
cd examples/<example folder>
NRPCoreSim -c simulation_config_docker.json
\endcode

<b><em>NOTE:</em></b>

The IP addresses of the local and remote hosts have to be taken into account when setting the experiment configuration parameters.
For example, in the case of launching a PythonJSONEngine:

- `"DockerDaemonAddress"` is the IP address of the remote docker daemon.
- `"ServerAddress"` is the IP address of engine running inside of the docker container (in the remote host)
- `"RegistrationServerAddress"` is the IP address of NRPCore

Also it must be ensured that ports required to connect to the aforementioned IP addresses are accessible from the respective hosts.
*/
